   "analyst": "S2",
   "problem": "The final grouping uses `f.bad.fillna(False)`, which silently classifies missing weather observations as other-weather flights. This is not supported by the bad-weather definition. The initial `delay_min` calculation also fails to handle post-midnight departures, although S2 later replaces it with the corrected clock-based `delay2` calculation."
  },
  {
   "analyst": "S3",
   "problem": "The final calculation excludes missing-weather flights because pandas groupby drops NaN, while S1/S2 include them as 'other'; this inconsistent treatment is not documented. S3 also uses a -60-minute rollover threshold, whereas its notes/code do not justify that threshold from the documentation, and it does not validate/deduplicate weather keys. Its earlier calculation incorrectly adds 1440 to every negative clock difference, though the later calculation corrects this."
  }
 ],
 "disagreements": "All three report the same rates, 41.7% and 6.8%, but they do not consistently handle unmatched weather observations: S1 and S2 count them as 'other', whereas S3 drops them. Their rollover thresholds also differ (-12 hours in S1/S2 versus -1 hour in S3), although the reported result apparently does not change. S3's stated counts (10/24 and 10/148) imply that only matched-weather completed flights are used, which conflicts with S1/S2's treatment of missing weather.",
